   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to extending\nthe authority of the department of environmental conservation to manage\nscallops",
    "basePrintNo": "S7064",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7064-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §13-0327, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7064\n\nSPONSOR: THOMAS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to\nextending the authority of the department of environmental conservation\nto manage scallops\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to extend the authority of the Department of\nEnvironmental Conservation (DEC) to manage scallops.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of this bill would amend Environmental Conservation Law (ECL)\n§ 13-0327 to extend, until December 31, 2024, DEC's authority to fix by\nregulation measures for the management of scallops (Family Pectinidae).\n\nSection 2 of this bill provides that the bill shall take effect imme-\ndiately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nDEC's regulatory authority for scallops is necessary in order to\npreserve the current regulatory program. Failure to extend the existing\nregulatory authority by December 31, 2021 will result in the loss of\nregulatory protection for scallops. The current regulatory program for\nscallops, mainly bay scallops, includes management measures that estab-\nlish a legal size limit of two and one quarter inches in height and an\nannual growth line, an open season from the first Monday in November\nthrough March 31, sublegal size tolerance and a possession limit of no\nmore than two percent of the catch when unavoidably taken, catch limits\nat no more than ten bushels per person or twenty per boat when two\nlicensed diggers are present, gear restrictions which allows the use of\na dredge or scrape having an opening of less than 36 inches in width\nthat may be brought onboard the vessel by hand, restrictions on\npossession and sale of scallops legally taken from the waters of the\nState, and provisions for scallop salvage and relay permits.\n\nFailure to extend this authority would result in the uncontrolled\nharvest of bay scallops of any size which are presently at very low\npopulation levels and would not be protective of the resource and\nshellfish industry. This regulatory authority provides DEC with the\nflexibility to respond to biological and environmental events such as\nbrown tide that have a significant impact on the viability of the bay\nscallop resource. This authority can also be used to develop management\nmeasures to support bay scallop restoration efforts that are taking\nplace in the State waters of Peconic Bays and in town waters.\n\nNew York's bay scallop resource is primarily located in the waters of\nPeconic and Gardiners Bays on the eastern end of Long Island. Bay scal-\nlop populations experienced a significant decline after 1985 due to the\noccurrence and repeated blooms of an unusual alga, Aureococcus anapha-\ngeffrens, also known as brown tide in the east end ays of the Peconics.\nAlthough the brown tide has not occurred in Peconic Bays since the\n1990s, the bay scallop population remains at low levels but has been\nstable in recent years. DEC's regulations were adopted to delay the\nopening of the boy scallop season in order to allow additional time for\nscallops to mature and reproduce and recover from the effects of the\nbrown tide before they were taken.\n\nThe current regulatory program provides for additional protections for\njuvenile \"bug\" scallops by requiring all scallops to be at least two and\none-quarter inches in height (middle of hinge to middle of bill) and\npossess an annual growth line (ring) prior to. harvest. These management\nmeasures allow for spawning potential to be maximized and ensures that\nall scallops will be able to spawn at least once prior to harvest. The\nbay scallop populations are at low levels in comparison to historic\nabundance that restoration and management measures are necessary to\nre-establish a sustainable fishery in the Peconic Bays and other waters.\nThe ongoing restoration efforts and management measures have shown posi-\ntive impacts on the bay scallop resource which is reflected in the\nincrease in commercial shellfish landings since 2009. This resource\nsupports an important commercial fishery primarily located in Peconic\nBays that provides economic benefits to the local economy and fishermen.\nThe bay scallop resource also provides opportunities for recreational\nharvest that are part of a maritime tradition on the east end of Long\nIsland.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nDEC's regulatory authority to manage scallops was last extended by Chap-\nter 256 of the Laws of 2018.\n\n \nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
